[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully. This is a question in number theory / [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mautomorphic forms.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me understand the question. A Hecke L-function is associated to a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHecke character (Größencharakter) of a number field. An Artin L-function is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124massociated to a Galois representation (a finite-dimensional complex [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation of the Galois group).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: if all local eigenvalues (of the Hecke L-function) are roots of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munity, is the Hecke L-function necessarily an Artin L-function?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about what "local eigenvalues" means here. For a Hecke L-function [0m
[38;2;124;124;124massociated to a Hecke character χ of a number field K, the local factors at [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munramified primes p are of the form:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mL_p(s, χ) = (1 - χ(𝔭) N(𝔭)^{-s})^{-1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfor a degree-1 Hecke L-function (associated to a Hecke character of K).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMore generally, a Hecke L-function can be associated to a Hecke character of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtype A_0 (algebraic Hecke character), and the local eigenvalues are the values [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mχ(𝔭) at unramified primes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me reconsider. The question is about Hecke L-functions in general. A [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHecke L-function is the L-function of a Hecke character (a grössencharakter). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThese are degree-1 L-functions over a number field K (or more precisely, they [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare associated to characters of the idele class group).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider the scope. "Hecke L-function" typically refers to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe L-function of a Hecke character (Größencharakter). This is a degree n L-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction where n = [K:Q] for a Hecke character of K... no wait.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me be more careful. A Hecke character χ of a number field K gives rise to an[0m[38;2;124;124;124mL-function:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mL(s, χ) = ∏_𝔭 (1 - χ(𝔭) N(𝔭)^{-s})^{-1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhere the product is over prime ideals of K. This is a degree [K:Q] L-function [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(when viewed as an L-function over Q).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the "local eigenvalues" are the values χ(𝔭) at unramified primes 𝔭.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question asks: if all these χ(𝔭) are roots of unity, is L(s, χ) an Artin L-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAn Artin L-function is associated to a representation ρ of Gal(K̄/Q) (or more [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgenerally Gal(K̄/K)). For a 1-dimensional Artin representation (i.e., a Dirichlet[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcharacter or more generally a character of the Galois group), the Artin L-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction equals a Hecke L-function.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key theorem here is: A Hecke character χ has finite order (i.e., χ takes [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mvalues in roots of unity) if and only if it is a character of the Galois group, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mi.e., the associated L-function is an Artin L-function (of a 1-dimensional [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation).[0m

